      "text": "To find the remainder when the sum of the first five primes is divided by the sixth prime, we can use modular arithmetic and break down the problem step by step.\n\nThe first five prime numbers are written as follows:\n\n2, 3, 5, 7, 11\n\nNow add the first five prime numbers:\n\n2 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 11 = 28\n\nNext, we have the sixth prime number. The first six prime numbers are: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, and 13. So we will calculate the modulo of 28 with the sixth prime number (13).\n\nUsing the modulo operator, we have:\n\n28 mod 13 = 28 - (13 * remainder)\nsimilarly, we get:\n\n6 = (13 * 5) - 1\n\n28 = 13 * 2 + 2\n\n35 = 13 * 2 + 9\n\nWe can see that 6 is a factor of 28, so the remainder will be 6. Therefore, when the sum of the first five primes is divided by the sixth prime, the answer will be 6 (remainder).\n\nFinal answer: 6",
